    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Religious conservatives, like Catholic theologian John Finnis, argue that the moral principle of double effect is at play, where the intention to cause death is morally wrong, but the unintended consequence of alleviating suffering is morally justifiable. They also invoke the concept of the \"intrinsic value of human life,\" which holds that every human being has inherent dignity and worth, regardless of their suffering. This is rooted in Catholic teaching on the inviolability of human life, as outlined in the Catechism of the Catholic Church. In contrast, religious liberals, like theologian Peter Singer, rely on the principle of consequentialism, arguing that the moral value of an action is determined by its consequences, and that physician-assisted dying can be a morally justifiable response to suffering, as it respects the individual's autonomy and alleviates their suffering.",
